Turning points, sheets, and planes
----------------------------------
For e=-3/2+e1/u+O(u^-2), c_h=4 delta^2-4 delta+3/4,

 P_u(y)=1-2(1-delta)y/u+(2e+c_h/(4u^2))y^2-2y^3.

With q=u^-1/2:

 y_+/-=-1 +/- v q + w q^2+O(q^3),
 v^2=2(delta-1-e1)/3,
 w=(-delta+4e1+1)/9,

 y_s=1/2+(2delta+e1-2)/(9u)+O(u^-2).

The six x-plane zeros are the two roots near i, their negatives near -i,
and the simple roots near +/-1/sqrt(2).  Opposite x roots exchange the
t-cover sheet.  In t=u^(1/4)x they scale by u^(1/4); in z=t^2=u^(1/2)y
opposite roots project together.  The negative-y pairs project to two
nearby negative-z points and the simple x pair to one positive-z point.
There is a pole of order six at x=t=z=0.  Infinity is regular and Q_u tends
to -8.

Stokes/equal-modulus curves are level curves of the real part of action
differences and anti-Stokes/constant-phase curves are the corresponding
imaginary-part level curves, always on a fixed sqrt(Q_u) sheet.  A half-turn
x -> -x exchanges the formal origin exponentials.  The physical
continuation cannot be selected from the negative displacement alone.

Domain, cuts, branches, and paths
---------------------------------
The only proved domain is a simply connected local cut neighbourhood of one
split pair alpha_u,beta_u near i.  It excludes x=0, the two zeros near -i,
the two simple zeros near +/-1/sqrt(2), and a cut joining the selected pair.
On it sqrt(Q_u) and the action are single-valued.  Compatible orientation
maps alpha_u,beta_u to -a_u,+a_u; reversing orientation or the square-root
branch reverses the corresponding action sign.

No domain approaching the two physical origin sectors has been proved
univalent.  Consequently no global progressive paths are asserted.  The
minimal missing lemma is a finite Weber--Airy--endpoint chain: prove
univalence of every action map, construct overlapping progressive paths,
track the origin half-turn and all gauge/cover monodromy, and prove every
transition operator is o(1), uniformly for fixed n and delta in a stated
compact set.  Airy regions may be needed if the selected physical chain
crosses a simple turning point.

Full comparison map and Liouville identity
-------------------------------------------

 Q_u=f+g/u+h/u^2,
 integral_alpha^x sqrt(Q_u) ds
   = integral_-a^zeta sqrt(v^2-a_u^2) dv,
 (pi i/2)a_u^2=integral_alpha^beta sqrt(Q_u) ds.

With W=(d zeta/dx)^(1/2)w:

 W_zetazeta=[u^2(zeta^2-a_u^2)+psi_u]W,
 psi_u=(dx/dzeta)^(1/2) d^2/dzeta^2 (dx/dzeta)^(-1/2).

For p=d zeta/dx, direct symbolic differentiation gives

 psi_u=p^(-1/2)d^2/dzeta^2 p^(1/2)
      =-3 p_x^2/(4p^4)+p_xx/(2p^3).

There is no untreated order-u remainder.

Endpoint maps
-------------
On the branch sqrt(Q_u)~2/x^3,

 sqrt(Q_u)=2/x^3-2(1-delta)/(ux)+O(x),
 integral^x sqrt(Q_u) ds=-1/x^2-2(1-delta)log(x)/u+O(1).

Changing the square-root branch changes both signs.  Changing the logarithm
branch adds a constant.  Since the Weber action is

 zeta^2/2-(a_u^2/2)log(zeta)+O(1),

the origin maps sectorwise by zeta~+/- i sqrt(2)/x.  Thus the two Weber
exponentials reproduce the two essential signs, but their algebraic powers
and identification with the compensated Bargmann germ remain dependent on
the missing gauge/cover monodromy calculation.

At x=infinity,

 sqrt(Q_u)=sqrt(-8)[1-(e/2+c_h/(16u^2))x^-2+O(x^-4)],
 integral^x sqrt(Q_u) ds=sqrt(-8)x+O(x^-1),
 zeta^2/2~sqrt(-8)x.

Both x=0 and x=infinity therefore reach different sectors of
zeta=infinity.  Together with the additional turning points this prevents a
one-domain claim.  Outer Bessel matching is not needed for the local origin
audit.  If later used, exact-to-Bessel error and Bessel truncation error must
remain separate.

Weber model and exact formula
-----------------------------
For Z=sqrt(2u) zeta,

 W_ZZ=[Z^2/4-p_u-1/2]W,
 p_u+1/2=u a_u^2/2.

DLMF 12.2.4 fixes the equation convention; DLMF 12.2.5 gives
D_p(Z)=U(-p-1/2,Z).  DLMF 12.2.19 gives

 U(a,Z)=+/- i exp(+/- i pi a)U(a,-Z)
  +sqrt(2pi) exp[+/- i pi(a/2-1/4)]
    U(-a,+/- iZ)/Gamma(1/2+a).

Putting a=-p-1/2 yields the model factor 1/Gamma(-p), with zeros
p=0,1,2,... .  DLMF 12.2.11 supplies the related Wronskian.  These are model
facts only: no physical canonical Weber solution was selected because the
origin-sector rotations and normalizations are not globally fixed.

Error control and zero localization
------------------------------------
The exact local variation-of-constants equation retained in the manuscript
uses a canonical Weber pair U1,U2, their Wronskian, and R_u=psi_u.  Its
conditional absolute envelope bound has variation

 V_u=integral_Gamma K_u(zeta,v)|R_u(v)||dv|,
 |epsilon| <= M_U[exp(V_u)-1].

This absolute form is meaningful at zeros of U.  Neither a global Gamma nor
V_u=o(1) has been proved, especially at the pole endpoint zeta=infinity.
Hence no absolute or relative global connection-coefficient error order is
available.  In particular the target C=A/Gamma(-p_u)+R has not been derived,
and no Rouche, implicit-function, or eigenvalue localization disk is valid.

Bargmann entireness
-------------------
The compensated and essential branches have only been matched at the level
of endpoint exponential signs.  Existence and uniqueness of the required
canonical sectorial representatives, patching to a holomorphic germ,
algebraic monodromy cancellation, entire continuation, symmetry reduction,
and equality of zero sets with an Olver coefficient remain unproved.

The exact condition

 Delta_WI(2eta,2delta,-2E/V,2eta)=0

is retained solely as the independently proved entireness determinant.  No
identity, equality of zeros, or asymptotic zero correspondence with an Olver
coefficient is claimed.

Energy
------
For fixed n only,

 E_n/V=-3 eta^(4/3)/2
  +[delta+sqrt(3)(n+1/2)-1]eta^(2/3)+O(1).

The -3/2 coefficient is rigorous from the double-root geometry.  The second
coefficient remains conditional on the missing physical global connection.
There is no proved eigenvalue error or localization disk and no uniformity
for growing n.  The corrected displaced-oscillator check uses
Omega^2=(2r^2+delta)^2-r^4 and is consistency evidence only; it was not used
as proof and no O(1) coefficient was extracted.

Sources and applicability
-------------------------
- F. W. J. Olver, Asymptotics and Special Functions, Chapter 12,
  Sections 1--4: two coalescing turning points after appropriate comparison
  domains and progressive paths have been supplied.
- NIST DLMF 2.8(vi): parabolic-cylinder classification for two coalescing
  turning points; it does not construct this complex pole-endpoint domain.
- NIST DLMF 12.2.4, 12.2.5, 12.2.11, 12.2.19: equation convention,
  D_p/U relation, Wronskian, and exact connection formula.

The first failed hypothesis is the comparison-domain/univalence hypothesis,
before any small-variation theorem can be invoked.

